1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,032
Welcome to Data two oh nine, Advanced Exploratory Data Analysis.

2
00:00:05,032 --> 00:00:10,692
In these first two lectures we build the foundation of the whole course:

3
00:00:10,692 --> 00:00:15,409
how to move from raw data to numbers you can actually trust.

4
00:00:15,409 --> 00:00:21,384
We start with why we explore data, then look at sampling, bias and variance.

5
00:00:22,184 --> 00:00:25,328
Over two lectures we cover six ideas.

6
00:00:25,328 --> 00:00:34,589
The role of E D A in the data science lifecycle, exploratory versus confirmatory analysis, and sampling bias.

7
00:00:34,589 --> 00:00:43,596
Then summary statistics, the shape of a distribution, and robust statistics like the median and the I Q R.

8
00:00:43,596 --> 00:00:49,544
This video covers the first three, plus the idea of bias and variance.

9
00:00:50,344 --> 00:00:57,839
John Tukey introduced exploratory data analysis in nineteen seventy seven as its own discipline.

10
00:00:57,839 --> 00:01:07,287
The idea is to look at data with an open mind, using pictures and resistant summaries, so that the data can surprise you.

11
00:01:07,287 --> 00:01:14,704
Think of E D A as detective work, and confirmatory analysis as the trial that comes afterwards.

12
00:01:15,504 --> 00:01:18,197
E D A is not stage two of a project.

13
00:01:18,197 --> 00:01:20,890
It runs through the whole lifecycle.

14
00:01:20,890 --> 00:01:32,860
Every arrow going into the exploration band is a question you answer by looking at data, and every arrow coming out is a decision you can defend in your report.

15
00:01:32,860 --> 00:01:39,144
You explore when you collect, when you clean, when you model, and when you evaluate.

16
00:01:39,944 --> 00:01:43,814
Exploration gives you five concrete things.

17
00:01:43,814 --> 00:01:44,444
Sanity:

18
00:01:44,444 --> 00:01:49,124
does the file contain what the documentation claims?

19
00:01:49,124 --> 00:01:49,844
Quality:

20
00:01:49,844 --> 00:01:53,984
missing values, duplicates, impossible values.

21
00:01:53,984 --> 00:01:54,884
Structure:

22
00:01:54,884 --> 00:01:58,754
shapes, relationships, clusters and trends.

23
00:01:58,754 --> 00:02:01,544
Hypotheses worth testing later.

24
00:02:01,544 --> 00:02:10,544
And modelling decisions, such as transformations, features, and what a sensible baseline looks like.

25
00:02:11,344 --> 00:02:13,924
Here is five minutes of looking.

26
00:02:13,924 --> 00:02:23,440
A quick describe call on seven hundred sixty eight patient records shows minimum values that are medically impossible:

27
00:02:23,440 --> 00:02:26,988
a glucose of zero, a blood pressure of zero.

28
00:02:26,988 --> 00:02:29,971
These were never missing in the file.

29
00:02:29,971 --> 00:02:32,955
Missingness had been encoded as zero.

30
00:02:32,955 --> 00:02:39,003
A model trained on this untouched would learn that no test predicts health.

31
00:02:39,003 --> 00:02:44,728
No algorithm tuning recovers from that, but five minutes of E D A does.

32
00:02:45,528 --> 00:02:50,544
These are two different jobs with two different sets of rules.

33
00:02:50,544 --> 00:02:58,797
Exploratory analysis asks what is going on, allows unlimited looks, and produces candidate hypotheses.

34
00:02:58,797 --> 00:03:08,425
Confirmatory analysis asks whether one specific claim is supported, gets one pre-specified look, and produces p-values.

35
00:03:08,425 --> 00:03:12,713
The fatal mistake in exploration is refusing to look.

36
00:03:12,713 --> 00:03:19,752
The fatal mistake in confirmation is testing a hypothesis that the same data suggested.

37
00:03:20,552 --> 00:03:23,317
Professionals follow four habits.

38
00:03:23,317 --> 00:03:29,935
First, split the data and hold out a confirmation set before you plot anything.

39
00:03:29,935 --> 00:03:34,041
Second, write the hypothesis down before testing.

40
00:03:34,041 --> 00:03:39,905
Third, label every claim in the report as exploratory or confirmatory.

41
00:03:39,905 --> 00:03:46,356
Fourth, adjust for multiple testing when one dataset has to carry many tests.

42
00:03:46,356 --> 00:03:52,640
In this course, your final project must label each headline claim this way.

43
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:57,230
Four things people call the data are not the same.

44
00:03:57,230 --> 00:04:01,247
The target population is everyone the claim is about.

45
00:04:01,247 --> 00:04:05,795
The sampling frame is the list you could actually draw from.

46
00:04:05,795 --> 00:04:12,238
The sample is the rows you ended up with, and the estimate is the number you compute.

47
00:04:12,238 --> 00:04:16,407
The gap between population and frame is coverage error.

48
00:04:16,407 --> 00:04:25,048
The Online Shoppers data has no row for a person who never visited, so it says nothing about why they stayed away.

49
00:04:25,848 --> 00:04:30,597
A sample can stop representing the population in six ways.

50
00:04:30,597 --> 00:04:36,246
Selection bias, self-selection, and survivorship bias in the top row.

51
00:04:36,246 --> 00:04:42,223
Non-response bias, undercoverage, and measurement bias in the bottom row.

52
00:04:42,223 --> 00:04:51,720
For example, product reviews come from delighted and furious customers, while the indifferent majority stays silent.

53
00:04:52,520 --> 00:04:54,151
Two cases to remember.

54
00:04:54,151 --> 00:05:07,346
In nineteen thirty six, the Literary Digest mailed ten million ballots and got over two million replies, yet predicted the wrong winner, because its address lists skewed wealthy.

55
00:05:07,346 --> 00:05:17,279
And in the Second World War, Abraham Wald pointed out that bullet holes on returning aircraft showed where planes could survive a hit.

56
00:05:17,279 --> 00:05:21,282
The missing rows were the planes that never came back.

57
00:05:21,282 --> 00:05:22,098
Always ask:

58
00:05:22,098 --> 00:05:26,768
what would a row look like that could never have been recorded?

59
00:05:27,568 --> 00:05:29,756
Now a second big idea:

60
00:05:29,756 --> 00:05:32,143
two ways of being wrong.

61
00:05:32,143 --> 00:05:34,331
Bias is where you aim.

62
00:05:34,331 --> 00:05:37,912
Variance is how steady your hand is.

63
00:05:38,712 --> 00:05:41,512
One picture holds the whole idea.

64
00:05:41,512 --> 00:05:46,602
Low bias and low variance means tight darts on the bullseye.

65
00:05:46,602 --> 00:05:50,589
High bias means tight darts in the wrong place.

66
00:05:50,589 --> 00:05:54,577
High variance means darts scattered everywhere.

67
00:05:54,577 --> 00:06:00,431
The top right, high bias and high variance, is the worst place to be:

68
00:06:00,431 --> 00:06:02,976
wrong, and confident about it.

69
00:06:03,776 --> 00:06:05,907
Bias is systematic error.

70
00:06:05,907 --> 00:06:13,921
You are off in the same direction every time, like a scale that reads two hundred grams heavy.

71
00:06:13,921 --> 00:06:16,820
It does not shrink with more data.

72
00:06:16,820 --> 00:06:18,951
Variance is random error.

73
00:06:18,951 --> 00:06:22,873
Two samples of fifty give two different means.

74
00:06:22,873 --> 00:06:29,096
It does shrink as data grows, roughly like one over the square root of n.

75
00:06:29,896 --> 00:06:36,096
This chart shows error as the sample grows from one hundred to ten thousand.

76
00:06:36,096 --> 00:06:39,441
The random error curve falls, but slowly:

77
00:06:39,441 --> 00:06:42,296
four times the data only halves it.

78
00:06:42,296 --> 00:06:44,418
The bias line never moves.

79
00:06:44,418 --> 00:06:51,352
Past a point, collecting more data only shrinks the error you were not worried about.

80
00:06:52,152 --> 00:07:01,465
In sampling, bias means the sample is systematically unlike the population, for example when only satisfied customers reply.

81
00:07:01,465 --> 00:07:11,230
Variance means the sample is fair but small, so fifty customers give three point nine stars and another fifty give four point two.

82
00:07:11,230 --> 00:07:21,144
A big biased sample is more dangerous than a small random one, because a narrow margin of error makes the wrong answer look precise.

83
00:07:21,944 --> 00:07:24,548
The same words describe models.

84
00:07:24,548 --> 00:07:27,656
A model that is too simple underfits:

85
00:07:27,656 --> 00:07:29,672
high bias, low variance.

86
00:07:29,672 --> 00:07:32,864
A model that is too flexible overfits:

87
00:07:32,864 --> 00:07:41,348
it passes through every point, including the noise, so different data gives a wildly different model.

88
00:07:41,348 --> 00:07:43,952
The best model sits in between.

89
00:07:43,952 --> 00:07:51,008
Same ten data points; the only thing that changed is how much freedom the model had.

90
00:07:51,808 --> 00:07:59,445
Expected prediction error equals bias squared, plus variance, plus irreducible noise.

91
00:07:59,445 --> 00:08:02,410
Bias is fixed by adding capacity.

92
00:08:02,410 --> 00:08:05,824
Variance is fixed by removing freedom:

93
00:08:05,824 --> 00:08:09,778
regularisation, pruning, more training data.

94
00:08:09,778 --> 00:08:12,922
Noise cannot be fixed by any model.

95
00:08:12,922 --> 00:08:16,696
It sets the floor on how good you can get.

96
00:08:17,496 --> 00:08:19,329
Here is the trade-off.

97
00:08:19,329 --> 00:08:23,412
On the left, simple models, where bias dominates.

98
00:08:23,412 --> 00:08:28,078
On the right, flexible models, where variance dominates.

99
00:08:28,078 --> 00:08:36,744
The total error you actually pay is U-shaped, and cross-validation is how you find the bottom of that U.

100
00:08:37,544 --> 00:08:40,236
How do you tell which one you have?

101
00:08:40,236 --> 00:08:47,928
High bias shows high training error and high test error that is close to it; more data barely helps.

102
00:08:47,928 --> 00:08:55,158
High variance shows very low training error and much higher test error; more data helps a lot.

103
00:08:55,158 --> 00:08:56,312
The quick test:

104
00:08:56,312 --> 00:09:02,696
does the gap between training and test error worry you more than the level of both?

105
00:09:03,496 --> 00:09:04,501
One caution.

106
00:09:04,501 --> 00:09:08,686
The trade-off is about estimation, not collection.

107
00:09:08,686 --> 00:09:16,387
Regularisation, cross-validation and more training rows all work on error inside your model.

108
00:09:16,387 --> 00:09:22,833
None of them repairs a sample that never contained the people you care about.

109
00:09:22,833 --> 00:09:27,688
Sampling bias is fixed at the design stage, or not at all.

110
00:09:28,488 --> 00:09:29,815
Remember this:

111
00:09:29,815 --> 00:09:33,036
bias repeats, variance reshuffles.

112
00:09:33,036 --> 00:09:38,343
More data shrinks variance and leaves bias where it was.

113
00:09:38,343 --> 00:09:42,796
In models, complexity trades one for the other.

114
00:09:42,796 --> 00:09:46,776
In samples, only better design fixes bias.

115
00:09:47,576 --> 00:09:48,347
To recap:

116
00:09:48,347 --> 00:09:52,115
E D A is a habit that recurs at every stage.

117
00:09:52,115 --> 00:09:59,138
Exploration generates hypotheses; confirmation tests them, never on the same rows.

118
00:09:59,138 --> 00:10:04,704
And sample size fixes noise, while only sample design fixes bias.

119
00:10:04,704 --> 00:10:12,326
Before the next class, load the Online Shoppers dataset and write the two-blank sentence:

120
00:10:12,326 --> 00:10:19,520
this sample can support claims about, blank, and cannot support claims about, blank.

